# Copy to `.env` at the repository root. One file, two readers:
# docker-compose.yml reads the `.env` beside it, and backend/app/config.py
# anchors to this directory rather than to the directory it was launched from.
#
# There used to be a second copy in backend/.env carrying DATABASE_URL, with
# this password written again inside it. They could disagree, and the symptom
# was an authentication failure that named neither file. DATABASE_URL is now
# derived from POSTGRES_PASSWORD - set it explicitly only to point at a
# database that is not the Compose one.
#
# `.env` is git-ignored and this file must never carry a real value. The
# password this replaced was committed to a public repository; it guarded a
# loopback-bound container of synthetic data, so the exposure mattered less
# than the habit.
#
# Any value works - the database is local, disposable, and rebuilt by
# `docker compose down -v && docker compose up -d`. Generate one with:
#
#     python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Optional. Without it `build_extractor` returns NullExtractor and the system
# behaves exactly as it did before value extraction existed - documents store
# and index, values stay absent, questions read PARTIAL. That is what makes
# introducing a model reversible, and it is why CI sets no key: the fallback
# path is the one CI exercises, at no cost and with no external dependency.
#
# With it set, chunk text goes to api.deepseek.com, outside Malaysia -
# permitted for the demo by the owner's 2026-08-27 ruling (AGENTS.md 3.1) ON
# THE CONDITION that no real customer document is uploaded while it is set.
#
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-...
